docker volume is a folder on host machine thats connected to a path/folder in a container
=> container can read or write to this volume
Thus, volumes are used to persist storage or data written / created in containers

Data storage is of 2 types:
1.volumes
2.bind mounts

1.volumes:
We specify the path in container and at max can give the volume name however , wheere this folder is careted
on host machine is managed by docker

is of 2 types => a) anonymous volumes b) named volumes

a) 
1. anonymous volumes are attached to a container. when a container is started or run , 
an anonymous volume is attached, and conatiner writes or reads from this 
2. on removing a conatiner (--rm) , anonymous containers are removed, on starting again, data is lost
3. if we do not start conatiner or un conatiner with --rm flag, on removing a conatiner, anonymous 
volumes are not removed, and thus piling up of volumes start
4. can be removed via:
        docker volume rm vol_name
        docker volume prune
5. for anonymous volumes, another line in dockerfile needs to be written specifying the path inside conatiner
        VOLUME ["/app/feedback"]
        -v /app/feedback
6.Anonymous volumes are used when we need to over write files in container during bind mounts


b)
1. named volumes => continue to stay even if the conatiner is removed=> persistent storage
2. on restarting a conatiner , same volume needs to be specified
3. here, no additional commands are added in docker file rather during run command
        docker run --name ... -v volname:/app/feedback [vol_name:/container path]

2) Bind mounts:
1. it is used to actively update the container code with that of original source code,thus no need to build image everytime code is updated
2. here we manually map the host machine folder to conatiner machine folder
3. initial image is built on snapshot of original code, later on changes made are overwritten
4. this can lead to loss app specific installations being erased=> wtever is there in source is copied, since source say wont have node_modules , those arent present in later updates in container code
5. thats why anonymous volumes are used to copy these installations and later on write from volume into the container
6. syntax:
        -v "abs source path:\container path: ro" [all anonymous volumes overwrite]
        -v "abs soucrce path":\container path [anonymous volume]
        -v "C:\Users\Vishruta\vibha\learn_docker\node-feedback-app":\app\feedback  -v \app\feedback


NOTE: The container path uses forward slashes / because your Docker container is running Linux. Your host path uses Windows-style backslashes \.

Eg: 
docker run --rm -v feedback-vol:/app/feedback -v "C:\Users\Vishruta\vibha\learn_docker\node-feedback-app:/app" -v /app/node_modules --name feedback-cont -p 3000:3000 feedback-image:1
